# Annotations — reading them at runtime

## Write your own with `@interface`

```java
@interface Role {
    String value();
    int level() default 1;
}
@Role(value = "admin", level = 3)
class AdminPanel { }
```

## Retention — how long the note survives

- **`SOURCE`** — compiler discards it (`@Override`)
- **`CLASS`** — in the `.class`, not at runtime (default)
- **`RUNTIME`** — in the `.class` **and readable by reflection while running** ← frameworks need this

## Runtime retention + reflection = frameworks

```java
for (Method m : cls.getDeclaredMethods())
    if (m.isAnnotationPresent(Test.class))   // read the note
        m.invoke(instance);                   // …act on it
```

That loop *is* JUnit. Same shape: `@Autowired`, `@Entity`, `@JsonProperty`, `@GetMapping`.

**Annotations carry intent; reflection reads it; the framework supplies behavior** — Java's declarative style.
